Critical data limitation: BELTUSPG Index is confirmed available only from 2006-12-31, limiting the usable sample for Factor 7 to approximately 2007–present. This is a short sample for evaluating 24- and 36-month forward return horizons. For longer history, the CBO's published potential GDP growth estimates (available from the CBO website and on FRED as GDPPOT) can be used as an external supplement, but these are revised estimates, not point-in-time vintages. The chapter's analysis likely uses a longer reconstructed series; users should treat any Bloomberg-only implementation of Factor 7 as a partial replication.
Point-in-time requirement: BELTUSPG Index does not carry confirmed vintage tracking on Bloomberg. For rigorous point-in-time construction, use CBO's published outlook documents (released approximately twice per year) and align each estimate to its publication date.
Evaluation horizons: For each horizon h ∈ {6M, 12M, 24M, 36M}, the regression is:
REAL_UST_RETURN(t, t+h) = α + β × REAL_YIELD_GAP(t) + ε(t)

Use Newey–West standard errors with lag truncation ≥ h/frequency to correct for overlapping return bias.
Part 5 — Nominal Yield Decomposition
The accounting decomposition is:
NOMINAL_YIELD_10Y(t) ≈ REAL_YIELD_10Y(t) + BREAKEVEN_10Y(t)

Using Bloomberg tickers:
GT10 Govt [yield] ≈ USGGT10Y Index [px_last] + USGGBE10 Index [px_last]

In changes:
Δ(GT10 yield) ≈ Δ(USGGT10Y) + Δ(USGGBE10)

Methodological note: This is an accounting identity derived from market prices, not an independent structural model. The decomposition attributes nominal yield changes to real-rate repricing versus inflation-compensation repricing. It does not identify the structural drivers of either component. Bloomberg's breakeven is computed as the yield difference between matched-maturity nominal Treasuries and TIPS; it includes both inflation expectations and an inflation risk premium, which are not separately observable from market prices alone.
